[LINK: (Homininae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homininae]

sister:  [LINK: (Hylobatidae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gibbon]

Pongidae Elliot, 1913

Gorillidae Frechkop, 1943

Panidae Ciochon, 1983

The Hominidae ( [LINK: (/hɒˈmɪnɪdiː/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English] ; hominids  [LINK: (/ˈhɒmɪnɪdz/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English] ), whose members are known as the great apes, [LINK: ([note 1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-5]  are a taxonomic  [LINK: (family) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Family_(biology)]  of  [LINK: (primates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primate]  that includes eight  [LINK: (extant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neontology#Extant_taxa_versus_extinct_taxa]  species in four  [LINK: (genera) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genus] :  [LINK: (Pongo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orangutan]  (the  [LINK: (Bornean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bornean_orangutan] ,  [LINK: (Sumatran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumatran_orangutan]  and  [LINK: (Tapanuli orangutan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tapanuli_orangutan] );  [LINK: (Gorilla) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorilla]  (the  [LINK: (eastern) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_gorilla]  and  [LINK: (western gorilla) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_gorilla] );  [LINK: (Pan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan_(genus)]  (the  [LINK: (chimpanzee) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimpanzee]  and the  [LINK: (bonobo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonobo] ); and  [LINK: (Homo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homo] , of which only  [LINK: (modern humans (Homo sapiens)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human]  remain. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-MSW3-1]

Numerous revisions in classifying the great apes have caused the use of the term hominid to change over time. The original meaning of "hominid" referred only to humans (Homo) and their closest extinct relatives. However, by the 1990s humans and other apes were considered to be "hominids".

The earlier restrictive meaning has now been largely assumed by the term  [LINK: (hominin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominini] , which comprises all members of the human clade after the split from the chimpanzees (Pan). The current meaning of "hominid" includes all the great apes including humans. Usage still varies, however, and some scientists and laypersons still use "hominid" in the original restrictive sense; the scholarly literature generally shows the traditional usage until the turn of the 21st century. [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-6]

Within the taxon Hominidae, a number of extant and extinct genera are grouped with the humans, chimpanzees, and gorillas in the subfamily  [LINK: (Homininae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homininae] ; others with orangutans in the subfamily  [LINK: (Ponginae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponginae]  (see  [LINK: (classification graphic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#Homgraph]  below). The  [LINK: (most recent common ancestor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Most_recent_common_ancestor]  of all Hominidae lived roughly 14 million years ago, [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-Hill&Ward1988-7]  when the ancestors of the orangutans speciated from the ancestral line of the other three genera. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-dawkins-tale-8]  Those ancestors of the family Hominidae had already speciated from the family  [LINK: (Hylobatidae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hylobatidae]  (the  [LINK: (gibbons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gibbon] ), perhaps 15 to 20 million years ago. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-dawkins-tale-8]  [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-9]

Due to the close genetic relationship between humans and the other great apes, certain  [LINK: (animal rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_rights]  organizations, such as the  [LINK: (Great Ape Project) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Ape_Project] , argue that nonhuman  [LINK: (great apes are persons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_ape_personhood]  and should be given basic  [LINK: (human rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Ape_Project#World_Declaration_on_Great_Apes] . Twenty-nine countries have instituted  [LINK: (research bans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Countries_banning_non-human_ape_experimentation]  to protect great apes from any kind of scientific testing. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-10]


# EVOLUTION #

In the early  [LINK: (Miocene) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miocene] , about 22 million years ago, there were many species of  [LINK: (tree-adapted) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree-dwelling]  primitive  [LINK: (catarrhines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catarrhini]  from East Africa; the variety suggests a long history of prior diversification. Fossils from 20 million years ago include fragments attributed to  [LINK: (Victoriapithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoriapithecus] , the earliest Old World monkey. Among the genera thought to be in the ape lineage leading up to 13 million years ago are  [LINK: (Proconsul) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proconsul_(mammal)] ,  [LINK: (Rangwapithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rangwapithecus] ,  [LINK: (Dendropithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dendropithecus] ,  [LINK: (Limnopithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Limnopithecus&action=edit&redlink=1] ,  [LINK: (Nacholapithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nacholapithecus] ,  [LINK: (Equatorius) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equatorius] ,  [LINK: (Nyanzapithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nyanzapithecus] ,  [LINK: (Afropithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afropithecus] , Heliopithecus, and  [LINK: (Kenyapithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenyapithecus] , all from East Africa.

At sites far distant from East Africa, the presence of other generalized non- [LINK: (cercopithecids) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_World_monkey] , that is, non-monkey catarrhines, of middle Miocene age— [LINK: (Otavipithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otavipithecus]  from cave deposits in Namibia, and  [LINK: (Pierolapithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierolapithecus]  and  [LINK: (Dryopithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dryopithecus]  from France, Spain and Austria—is further evidence of a wide diversity of ancestral ape forms across Africa and the Mediterranean basin during the relatively warm and equable climatic regimes of the early and middle Miocene. The most recent of these far-flung Miocene apes ( [LINK: (hominoids) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominoids] ) is  [LINK: (Oreopithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oreopithecus] , from the fossil-rich coal beds in northern Italy and dated to 9 million years ago.

Molecular evidence indicates that the lineage of gibbons (family Hylobatidae), the "lesser apes", diverged from that of the great apes some 18–12 million years ago, and that of orangutans (subfamily Ponginae) diverged from the other great apes at about 12 million years. There are no fossils that clearly document the ancestry of gibbons, which may have originated in a still-unknown South East Asian hominoid population; but fossil proto-orangutans, dated to around 10 million years ago, may be represented by  [LINK: (Sivapithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sivapithecus]  from India and  [LINK: (Griphopithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Griphopithecus]  from Turkey. [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-Srivastava2009-11]  Species close to the last common ancestor of gorillas, chimpanzees and humans may be represented by  [LINK: (Nakalipithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nakalipithecus]  fossils found in Kenya. Molecular evidence suggests that between 8 and 4 million years ago, first the gorillas (genus Gorilla), and then the chimpanzees (genus Pan) split off from the line leading to humans. Human  [LINK: (DNA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DNA]  is approximately 98.4% identical to that of chimpanzees when comparing single nucleotide polymorphisms (see  [LINK: (human evolutionary genetics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_evolutionary_genetics] ). [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-12]  The fossil record, however, of gorillas and chimpanzees is limited; both poor preservation—rain forest soils tend to be acidic and dissolve bone—and  [LINK: (sampling bias) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sampling_bias]  probably contribute most to this problem.

Other  [LINK: (hominins) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominins]  probably adapted to the drier environments outside the African equatorial belt; and there they encountered antelope, hyenas, elephants and other forms becoming adapted to surviving in the East African  [LINK: (savannas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Savannas] , particularly the regions of the  [LINK: (Sahel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahel]  and the  [LINK: (Serengeti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serengeti] . The wet equatorial belt contracted after about 8 million years ago, and there is very little fossil evidence for the divergence of the hominin lineage from that of gorillas and chimpanzees—which split was thought to have occurred around that time. The earliest fossils argued by some to belong to the human lineage are  [LINK: (Sahelanthropus tchadensis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sahelanthropus_tchadensis]  (7 Ma) and  [LINK: (Orrorin tugenensis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orrorin_tugenensis]  (6 Ma), followed by  [LINK: (Ardipithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardipithecus]  (5.5–4.4 Ma), with species Ar. kadabba and Ar. ramidus.


# TAXONOMY #

The classification of the great apes has been  [LINK: (revised several times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_hominoid_taxonomy]  in the last few decades; these revisions have led to a varied use of the word "hominid" over time. The original meaning of the term referred to only humans and their closest relatives—what is now the modern meaning of the term " [LINK: (hominin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominin] ". The meaning of the  [LINK: (taxon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxon]  Hominidae changed gradually, leading to a modern usage of "hominid" that includes all the great apes including humans.

A number of very similar words apply to related classifications:

A  [LINK: (hominoid) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominoid] , sometimes called an  [LINK: (ape) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ape] , is a member of the superfamily  [LINK: (Hominoidea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominoidea] : extant members are the gibbons ( [LINK: (lesser apes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lesser_ape] , family Hylobatidae) and the hominids.

A hominid is a member of the family Hominidae, the great apes: orangutans, gorillas, chimpanzees and humans.

A hominine is a member of the subfamily  [LINK: (Homininae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homininae] : gorillas, chimpanzees, and humans (excludes orangutans).

A hominin is a member of the tribe  [LINK: (Hominini) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominini] : chimpanzees and humans. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-Wood2010-13]

A homininan, following a suggestion by Wood and Richmond (2000), would be a member of the subtribe  [LINK: (Hominina) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominina]  of the tribe  [LINK: (Hominini) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominini] : that is, modern humans and their closest relatives, including  [LINK: (Australopithecina) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australopithecina] , but excluding chimpanzees. [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-WoodRichmond-14]  [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-15]

A human is a member of the genus Homo, of which Homo sapiens is the only extant species, and within that Homo sapiens sapiens is the only surviving  [LINK: (subspecies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subspecies] .

A cladogram indicating common names (cf.  [LINK: (more detailed cladogram below) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#Classification] ):

[LINK: (Hylobatidae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hylobatidae] gibbons

[LINK: (Pongo abelii) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pongo_abelii]

[LINK: (Pongo tapanuliensis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pongo_tapanuliensis]

[LINK: (Pongo pygmaeus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pongo_pygmaeus]

[LINK: (Gorilla gorilla) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorilla_gorilla]

[LINK: (Gorilla beringei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorilla_beringei]

[LINK: (Pan troglodytes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan_troglodytes]

[LINK: (Pan paniscus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan_paniscus]

[LINK: (Homo sapiens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homo_sapiens] humans

Hominidae was originally the name given to the family of humans and their (extinct) close relatives, with the other great apes (that is, the orangutans, gorillas and chimpanzees) all being placed in a separate family, the  [LINK: (Pongidae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pongidae] . However, that definition eventually made Pongidae  [LINK: (paraphyletic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraphyletic]  because at least one great ape species (the chimpanzees) proved to be more closely related to humans than to other great apes. Most taxonomists today encourage  [LINK: (monophyletic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monophyletic]  groups—this would require, in this case, the use of Pongidae to be restricted to just one closely related grouping. Thus, many  [LINK: (biologists) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biologist]  now assign Pongo (as the subfamily  [LINK: (Ponginae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponginae] ) to the family Hominidae. The taxonomy shown here follows the monophyletic groupings according to the modern understanding of human and great ape relationships.

Humans and close relatives including the tribes  [LINK: (Hominini) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominini]  and  [LINK: (Gorillini) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorillini]  form the subfamily  [LINK: (Homininae) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homininae]  (see classification graphic below). (A few researchers go so far as to refer the chimpanzees and the gorillas to the genus Homo along with humans.) [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-16]  [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-17]  [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-18]  But, those fossil relatives more closely related to humans than the chimpanzees represent the especially close members of the human family, and without necessarily assigning subfamily or tribal categories.[ [LINK: (clarification needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Please_clarify] ] [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-19]

Many extinct hominids have been studied to help understand the relationship between modern humans and the other extant hominids. Some of the extinct members of this family include  [LINK: (Gigantopithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigantopithecus] ,  [LINK: (Orrorin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orrorin] ,  [LINK: (Ardipithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ardipithecus] ,  [LINK: (Kenyanthropus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenyanthropus] , and the  [LINK: (australopithecines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australopithecine]   [LINK: (Australopithecus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australopithecus]  and  [LINK: (Paranthropus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paranthropus] . [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-20]

The exact criteria for membership in the tribe Hominini under the current understanding of human origins are not clear, but the taxon generally includes those  [LINK: (species) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Species]  that share more than 97% of their  [LINK: (DNA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DNA]  with the modern human  [LINK: (genome) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genome] , and exhibit a capacity for  [LINK: (language) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Language]  or for simple  [LINK: (cultures) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture]  beyond their "local family" or band. The  [LINK: (theory of mind) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theory_of_mind]  concept—including such faculties as empathy, attribution of mental state, and even empathetic deception—is a controversial criterion; it distinguishes the adult human alone among the hominids. Humans acquire this capacity after about four years of age, whereas it has not been proven (nor has it been disproven) that gorillas or chimpanzees ever develop a theory of mind. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-21]  This is also the case for some  [LINK: (New World monkeys) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_World_monkey]  outside the family of great apes, as, for example, the  [LINK: (capuchin monkeys) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capuchin_monkey#Theory_of_mind] .

However, even without the ability to test whether early members of the Hominini (such as  [LINK: (Homo erectus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homo_erectus] ,  [LINK: (Homo neanderthalensis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neanderthal] , or even the australopithecines) had a theory of mind, it is difficult to ignore similarities seen in their living cousins. Orangutans have shown the development of culture comparable to that of chimpanzees, [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-22]  and some[ [LINK: (who?) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Manual_of_Style/Words_to_watch#Unsupported_attributions] ] say the orangutan may also satisfy those criteria for the  [LINK: (theory of mind) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theory_of_mind]  concept. These scientific debates take on political significance for advocates of  [LINK: (great ape personhood) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_ape_personhood] .


# DESCRIPTION #

The great apes are tailless primates, with the smallest living species being the bonobo at 30 to 40 kilograms (66 to 88 lb) in weight, and the largest being the eastern gorillas, with males weighing 140 to 180 kilograms (310 to 400 lb). In all great apes, the males are, on average, larger and stronger than the females, although the degree of  [LINK: (sexual dimorphism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexual_dimorphism]  varies greatly among species. Hominid teeth are similar to those of the  [LINK: (Old World monkeys) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_World_monkey]  and gibbons, although they are especially large in gorillas. The  [LINK: (dental formula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dentition]  is 2.1.2.32.1.2.3. Human teeth and jaws are markedly smaller relative to body size compared to those of other apes. This may be an adaptation not only to the extensive use of tools, which has supplanted the role of jaws in hunting and fighting, but also to eating cooked food since the end of the  [LINK: (Pleistocene) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pleistocene] . [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-Brace&Mahler-23]  [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-Wrangham2007-24]


# BEHAVIOR #

Although most living species are predominantly  [LINK: (quadrupedal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadrupedal] , they are all able to use their hands for gathering food or nesting materials, and, in some cases, for tool use. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-EoM-25]  They build complex sleeping platforms, also called nests, in trees to sleep in at night, but chimpanzees and gorillas also build terrestrial nests, and gorillas can also sleep on the bare ground. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-26]

All species are  [LINK: (omnivorous) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omnivore] , [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-27]  although chimpanzees and orangutans primarily eat fruit. When gorillas run short of fruit at certain times of the year or in certain regions, they resort to eating shoots and leaves, often of  [LINK: (bamboo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo] , a type of grass. Gorillas have extreme adaptations for chewing and digesting such low-quality forage, but they still prefer fruit when it is available, often going miles out of their way to find especially preferred fruits. Humans, since the  [LINK: (Neolithic Revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neolithic_Revolution] , have consumed mostly  [LINK: (cereals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cereal]  and other  [LINK: (starchy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starch]  foods, including increasingly highly  [LINK: (processed foods) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Food_processing] , as well as many other  [LINK: (domesticated plants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domestication#Plants]  (including fruits) and  [LINK: (meat) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meat] .

Both chimpanzees and humans are known to wage  [LINK: (wars) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War]  over territories and resources. [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-28]

[LINK: (Gestation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gestation]  in great apes lasts 8–9 months, and results in the birth of a single offspring, or, rarely, twins. The young are born helpless, and require care for long periods of time. Compared with most other mammals, great apes have a remarkably long adolescence, not being  [LINK: (weaned) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weaning]  for several years, [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-Hamilton_2017-29]  and not becoming fully mature for eight to thirteen years in most species (longer in orangutans and humans). As a result, females typically give birth only once every few years. There is no distinct breeding season. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-EoM-25]

Gorillas and chimpanzees live in family groups of around five to ten individuals, although much larger groups are sometimes noted. Chimpanzees live in larger groups that break up into smaller groups when fruit becomes less available. When small groups of female chimpanzees go off in separate directions to forage for fruit, the dominant males can no longer control them and the females often mate with other subordinate males. In contrast, groups of gorillas stay together regardless of the availability of fruit. When fruit is hard to find, they resort to eating leaves and shoots.

This fact is related to gorillas' greater sexual dimorphism relative to that of chimpanzees; that is, the difference in size between male and female gorillas is much greater than that between male and female chimpanzees. This enables gorilla males to physically dominate female gorillas more easily. In both chimpanzees and gorillas, the groups include at least one dominant male, and young males leave the group at maturity.


# LEGAL STATUS #

Due to the close genetic relationship between humans and the other great apes, certain  [LINK: (animal rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_rights]  organizations, such as the  [LINK: (Great Ape Project) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Ape_Project] , argue that nonhuman  [LINK: (great apes are persons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_ape_personhood]  and, per the  [LINK: (Declaration on Great Apes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Declaration_on_Great_Apes] , should be given basic  [LINK: (human rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights] . In 1999, New Zealand was the first country to ban any great ape experimentation, and now 29 countries have currently instituted a  [LINK: (research ban) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_ape_research_ban]  to protect great apes from any kind of scientific testing.

On 25 June 2008, the Spanish parliament supported a new law that would make "keeping apes for circuses, television commercials or filming" illegal. [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-30]  On 8 September 2010, the  [LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union]   [LINK: (banned the testing of great apes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_ape_research_ban] . [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae#cite_note-31]


